This is my revised attempt at duplicating the "cloudfront with S3 bucket" example
that AWS offers at https://github.com/aws-samples/amazon-cloudfront-secure-static-site/tree/master/templates 

I would like to think this is both clearer and better explained.  In terms of the "better explained", I challenge myself to write a lot of commentary.

This is the target that creates everything - and can also strip it down if run as "--teardown"

(See also trial_bucket.dply, which does the same thing with primitives; this uses the cloudfront.distribution action
which wraps up creating the CachePolicy, OAC, RHP, CacheBehaviors and the bucket policy.)

	target cloudfront_from_bucket

The core of the solution is to have a bucket which has all of the files in it that we want

		ensure aws.S3.Bucket "news.consolidator.info" => consolidator_bucket
			@teardown delete

We need to copy some files into that, so we assume that there are files in a directory
indicated by ${CONSOLIDATOR_SRC}/IMAGE.  We find that directory (if it exists) and copy
the files into the bucket.

Get the value of the CONSOLIDATOR_SRC variable.  This will be a string

		env "CONSOLIDATOR_SRC" => root

We can now find the directory corresponding to that, which is also an active "file source",
so we can use it as the source of files for a copy action.

		files.dir root "IMAGE" => src_dir

Now copy the files from the src dir to the bucket.

		files.copy src_dir consolidator_bucket

Turning to the other end, it's a requirement that we have a domain name that we can use.  A lot of the example seems to revolve around whether
or not you want the domain to respond, or whether you just want a subdomain to respond (they call this "apex" which is apparently
official DNS terminology - the "root" domain).

		find aws.Route53.DomainName "consolidator.news" => domain

The domain needs a certificate.  In addition to the "www" subdomain, we want to create
an alias for the "apex" node.

This is touch-and-go complexity for doing something using ensure rather than stepping up to adding
a specific "certificate" verb.  The big clue is in the duplication.

		ensure aws.CertificateManager.Certificate "www.consolidator.news" => cert
			@teardown delete
			Domain <- domain
			SubjectAlternativeNames <- "consolidator.news" // The apex node
			ValidationMethod <- "DNS"

// TODO: it should be possible to do the same thing with other DNS providers
// eg. Dreamhost
// https://api.dreamhost.com/?key=1A2B3C4D5E6F7G8H&cmd=dns-add_record&record=example.com&type=TXT&value=test123

Now we are in a position to set up our cloudfront distribution, which will serve content on the named
site from the bucket we created earlier.

In the fullness of time, I want a "cloudfront" statement that combines the next 4 or 5 operations, but for now,
we can "hardcode" them using ensure.  These need to be created in the appropriate order, otherwise they aren't
there when they're needed.  I'm not sure of all the constraints, but this order definitely works.

We need a "CachePolicy" object

//		ensure aws.CloudFront.CachePolicy "cache-policy" => cp
//			@teardown delete
//			MinTTL <- 300

The "OriginAccessControl" specifies the relationship between Cloudfront and "the source", in our case S3

//		ensure aws.CloudFront.OriginAccessControl "access-s3-bucket" => oac
//			@teardown delete
//			OriginAccessControlOriginType <- "s3"
//			SigningBehavior <- "always"
//			SigningProtocol <- "sigv4"

We want to associate certain file extensions with certain MIME types, and to do that we need to define a
ResponseHeadersPolicy and embed it in a custom CachePolicy.  Yes, it really is this complicated.  In fairness,
this is in fact a much more powerful and general mechanism.

//		ensure aws.CloudFront.ResponseHeadersPolicy "html-mime-type" => rhp
//			@teardown delete
//			Header <- "Content-Type"
//			Value <- "text/html"

And to make it clear that we are only doing this for HTML files, this RHP is associated with a custom cache policy
for files ending in "*.html"

//		coin aws.CloudFront.CacheBehavior "customize-html-headers" => cb
//			@teardown delete
//			CachePolicy <- cp->id
//			PathPattern <- "*.html"
//			ResponseHeadersPolicy <- rhp->id
//			TargetOriginId <- "s3-bucket-target"

Finally, we can put all these parts together to build up the entire picture of the distribution.

		cloudfront.distribution.fromS3 "for-consolidator-news" => cloudfront
			@teardown delete
			Bucket <- consolidator_bucket
			Comment <- "a distribution for consolidator.news"
//			OriginDNS <- consolidator_bucket->dnsName
//			OriginAccessControl <- oac->id
			Certificate <- cert->arn
//			CachePolicy <- cp->id
			Domain <- []
				"www.consolidator.news"
				"consolidator.news"
			MinTTL <- 300
			CacheBehaviors <- []
				{}
					PathPattern <- "*.html"
					ResponseHeaders <- {}
						Header <- "Content-Type"
						Value <- "text/html"

//			CacheBehaviors <- [cb]
			TargetOriginId <- "s3-bucket-target"

//		policy => cloudfront_policy
//			allow aws.action.S3.GetObject (consolidator_bucket->allResources)
//				action aws.action.S3.PutObject
//				principal aws.principal.Service aws.principal.CloudFront
//				condition aws.cond.StringEquals aws.SourceArn (cloudfront->arn)


//		attachPolicy consolidator_bucket cloudfront_policy

Then we need to bind the CNAME(s) and/or aliases that we want to use to the cloudfront domain name

		ensure aws.Route53.CNAME "www.consolidator.news"
			@teardown delete
			PointsTo <- (cloudfront->domainName)
			Zone <- domain->zoneId


For the Apex node, we can't use a CNAME so we have to use an ALIAS instead

		ensure aws.Route53.ALIAS "consolidator.news"
			@teardown delete
			PointsTo <- (cloudfront->domainName)
			AliasZone <- "Z2FDTNDATAQYW2" // this is lifted from the documentation
			UpdateZone <- domain->zoneId
